 ### What a step does
 
-An engine can own a whole turn through `agent/execute-turn` after the first non-empty pre-step admission. A handling listener logs admitted input and its own steps, runs all model/tool/retry/stopping policy, and returns the turn reason; the default loop does none of that work. The host retains turn boundaries, cancellation, status, follow-up scheduling and teardown. `prepareInput(step)` admits later steering through the same prompt assembly and pre-step policy. A listener calling `next()` preserves the standard loop; a failed handler closes the turn without dispatching a fallback engine.
+An engine can own a whole turn through `agent/execute-turn` after the first non-empty pre-step admission. A handling listener logs admitted input and its own steps, runs all model/tool/retry/stopping policy, and returns the turn reason; the default loop does none of that work. The host retains turn boundaries, cancellation, status, follow-up scheduling and teardown. `prepareInput(step)` admits later steering through the same prompt assembly and pre-step policy. A listener calling `next()` preserves the standard loop; a failed handler without durable ownership closes the turn without dispatching a fallback engine.
+
+`agent.externalTurns` advertises `dsh.external-turn-host/v1`. A whole-turn engine claims `turn/owner` before its first step, with a versioned engine identifier and complete serialized recovery admission. The Host must flush this event before creating external execution state. The required event makes unsupported older readers refuse the log. Resume preserves an owned open turn without synthetic tool results or closers; ordinary turns and forks retain their existing repair. `pending()` grants no execution authority. A trusted Host resolves the original preset and calls `resume(engine, execute)` to reserve the same turn exclusively. Rejection keeps ownership open, parks follow-ups and reports the error; successful settlement closes it once before queued work can run. Later input still uses scoped assembly and pre-step policy. Neither the Session record nor a matching engine string grants tool permissions.
 
 Each step sends the session's derived history — with the latest non-empty `system/message` node as the effective prompt, or no system messages when the rendered prompt is empty — and its visible tool schemas; the model's tool calls run through the guarded tool pipeline and every accepted fact is appended to the session log before the next step derives from it. Parallel-safe calls may overlap up to `maxParallelToolCalls`; exclusive calls run alone as ordering barriers. Cancellation is cooperative: `agent.cancel()` aborts the current activity and, unless `keepInbox` is set, clears pending work; a cancelled stream finalizes the text already delivered to the user.
 
